.TH CBMAP_CREATE_FULL 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cbmap_create_full \- create a BST map with full customization
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap cbmap_create_full(ccol_data_type key_type,
                        ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mmgmt_procs,
                        ccol_comparison_proc_t custom_comparison_proc,
                        char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a self-balancing BST map. The caller has full control over the key
type, the memory management and the comparison function.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
key_type                 Type of the keys that the map stores
mmgmt_procs              Custom memory management, or NULL
custom_comparison_proc   Custom comparison function, or NULL
err                      Optional pointer that receives an error string
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a pointer to the new BST map on success
Returns NULL on failure
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Default comparison for ccol_char: the native char comparison of this
platform. It is signed or unsigned, to match the behavior of char on this
platform
Default comparison for ccol_signed_char and for short/int/long/long_long:
a signed comparison. It does not depend on the char signedness of this
platform. Note that ccol_signed_char is a scalar signed char or its typedef
int8_t. A signed char * string key is ccol_string instead
Default comparison for the unsigned integer types and ccol_pointer: an
unsigned comparison
Default comparison for ccol_float/double/long_double: a numeric
floating-point comparison. A NaN key sorts as greater than every non-NaN
key, and equal to every other NaN key
Default comparison for ccol_string: a strcmp-equivalent lexicographic order
For ccol_other_types (for example a struct key type) the default comparison
is a raw memcmp of the representation. That memcmp compares indeterminate
padding bytes. It also compares each pointer member by address, not by the
object that the pointer points at. Give custom_comparison_proc for a struct
key
Every default comparison above applies to two keys of the same stored size.
Only a key type of variable width can have two keys of different sizes.
Those key types are ccol_string and ccol_other_types. For two such keys, the
map compares the common prefix first. If the common prefix is equal, the map
compares the sizes, and the shorter key comes first. Every key of a
fixed-width key type is exactly ccol_fixed_width_data_type_size(3) bytes
wide. Every entry point that takes a key_pair needs that exact width. This
is also true when the caller gives a custom_comparison_proc. That function
gets two bare key pointers and no sizes, so it reads a width that the key
type fixes.
A custom comparison gets void* pointers to the key data. A key that
comes in through the raw layer can be at any address. Read a key that
is wider than one byte through memcpy. Do not read it through a pointer
cast to the key type
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_create (3),
.BR cbmap_create_mp (3),
.BR cbmap_create_ch (3),
.BR cbmap_destroy (3)
